<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cerignola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">28 April <a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/april-28">On this day: 28 April</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-war-of-1499-1504">Italian War of 1499–1504</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cerignola was fought on 28 April 1503 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. A Spanish army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> defeated the French under the Duke of Nemours, who was killed. Spanish arquebusiers firing from behind a ditch and rampart broke the French attacks, and it is often counted as the first battle won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> small arms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola.jpg" alt="Battle of Cerignola" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Federico de Madrazo y Kuntz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elgrancapitantrasbatalladeceri%C3%B1ola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Ferdinand II the Crown joined Castile in the conquest of Granada, completed in 1492, and Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba won Naples back from France at <b>Cerignola</b> and the Garigliano in 1503.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ferdinand II of Aragon agreed in 1500 to partition the kingdom, then fell out, and the Spanish commander Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, the 'Great Captain', won the <b>Battle of Cerignola</b>, one of the first battles decided largely by handguns, and the Battle of the Garigliano, both in 1503.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li></ul></section>
